WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.947
Kitchen cabinets that sell for several thousand dollars contain only a few hundred in sheet goods and hardware. This is First Principles Daily, Episode 132. A finished kitchen runs 20 to 30 of those cabinets. Each one is panels, fasteners, and hinges. The panels are commodity sheet goods. The spread above that stock is not fixed by physics. It comes from how the trades still sizes, finishes, and delivers every box. Stock, semi-custom, and custom cabinets follow the same basic sequence.

00:00:29.947 --> 00:00:35.187
A designer measures the room, then orders from a manufacturer or a local shop that cuts

00:00:35.187 --> 00:00:36.747
and assembles to those dimensions.

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:42.687
The panels are plywood, particle board, or medium density fiberboard, usually faced

00:00:42.687 --> 00:00:44.507
with melamine or a thin veneer.

00:00:44.927 --> 00:00:50.127
The JDI construction guide notes that particle board and medium density fiberboard stay flat

00:00:50.127 --> 00:00:54.147
and costs less than plywood, but they swell if water reaches an edge.

00:00:54.147 --> 00:00:57.727
Plie would hold screws better and resist occasional moisture.

00:00:58.227 --> 00:01:03.347
All three must meet the Environmental Protection Agency Toxic Substances Control Act Title

00:01:03.347 --> 00:01:06.547
6 for Maldehyde Limits that have applied since 2019.

00:01:07.487 --> 00:01:12.047
Once the boxes are built, they travel to the site, often weeks or months after the order.

00:01:13.017 --> 00:01:17.627
Instalers then shim, scribe, and fasten them to walls and floors that are rarely perfectly

00:01:17.627 --> 00:01:18.087
square.

00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:24.127
The NYSEA specification and the architectural woodwork standards both set minimum thickness

00:01:24.127 --> 00:01:29.027
and joint rules for the different grades. Every project still needs field adjustments.

00:01:29.587 --> 00:01:34.027
That split between factory and job site adds handling, storage, and scheduling.

00:01:34.667 --> 00:01:39.947
Custom work adds another step. Each cabinet is drawn, priced, and built as a one-off.

00:01:40.387 --> 00:01:44.467
So the same basic box can carry different markups depending on finish options

00:01:44.467 --> 00:01:48.587
and the number of drawers or doors. The Kitchen Cabinet Manufacturers

00:01:48.587 --> 00:01:55.847
association A161.1 Performance Standard, test completed cabinets for load, cycle life,

00:01:56.027 --> 00:01:56.827
and finished durability.

00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:00.587
That is why most new units meet basic strength requirements.

00:02:01.207 --> 00:02:04.487
Those tests do not address the cost of the supply chain itself.

00:02:05.207 --> 00:02:10.767
Cabinets are large, fragile, and empty, and ordered late in a remodel, so they tie up floor

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:12.407
space and capital for weeks.

00:02:13.087 --> 00:02:17.607
Builders and homeowners treat the resulting price as normal because every competitor uses

00:02:17.607 --> 00:02:22.967
is roughly the same process. The sequence starts with a room-specific cut list. Every

00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:28.147
panel is measured and sawed on its own, not produced in a repeating run. After cutting,

00:02:28.427 --> 00:02:33.107
each edge receives banding. Any visible wood surface moves through sanding and coating

00:02:33.107 --> 00:02:37.827
steps that change with the chosen grade. The finished box is then enter a distribution

00:02:37.827 --> 00:02:43.207
network that has to protect large hollow volumes and transit and in storage. When they reach

00:02:43.207 --> 00:02:46.067
Each the job site, crews spend additional hours fitting them.

00:02:46.647 --> 00:02:51.027
The original measurements were taken against walls that drywall or flooring tolerances have

00:02:51.027 --> 00:02:51.687
since altered.

00:02:52.387 --> 00:02:55.087
Each handoff multiplies labor and carrying cost.

00:02:55.787 --> 00:02:58.787
None of those handoffs changes the sheet goods inside the cabinets.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.980
Now put a magic wand on the same kitchen.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.380
If the wand only arranged the atoms, the floor would be the cost of the panels, the fasteners,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.160
the hinges, and the drawer slides.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:14.940
A typical base cabinet might use roughly 20-30 square feet of 3-quarter inch particle board

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:18.020
or plywood, plus a few dollars of hinges and screws.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.180
Commodity prices for those sheet goods move around.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.720
Even so, the raw panel costs for an average kitchen set of cabinets lands in the low hundreds

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:27.260
of dollars.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.760
Add another 100 or 2 for hardware and edge banding.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.460
That is the physical minimum, before any cutting, joining, finishing or transport.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:40.580
board. Take the installed price, several thousand dollars, and divide it by that material value.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:45.800
The result is an idiot index in the range of ten to twenty, or higher if more of the

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:51.220
job is custom. The index is not a precise measurement. It is a signal that most of the price

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:56.920
sits in the steps between raw panel and finished, installed box. Those steps start with sizing.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:02.460
Every kitchen triggers a new cut list because rooms differ, and because the industry still treats

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.360
cabinets as furniture rather than standardized modules.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:11.340
The architectural woodwork standards define economy, custom, and premium grades largely by

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.860
how much handwork is allowed, and by how many joints may show.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.440
Each grade adds labor and inspection.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:19.140
Next comes finishing.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:25.080
Even melamine-faced panels need edge banding, and any exposed wood needs sanding and coating.

00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:30.680
The supply chain then moves the finished boxes, often partially assembled, to a warehouse

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:36.040
or straight to the site. Carrying costs pile up while the cabinets wait for drywall, flooring,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:41.680
or appliances. On-site installation adds another round of measuring, shimming, and scribing,

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.340
because the boxes were built to nominal dimensions rather than to the actual walls. Each of those

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:53.720
stages multiplies the original material cost. None of them changes the atoms inside the panels. One

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:59.360
objection is that custom finishes or premium hardware justify the spread. Even when melamine

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.960
mean interiors and basic hinges run through the whole kitchen, the installed price remains

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:09.520
many times the panel and fastener cost. The extra dollars therefore trace less to material

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.840
upgrades than to the repeated custom measurement, the low volume cutting, the weeks of storage,

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:20.945
and the final on-site fitting, that the present workflow requires. A redesign would start

00:05:20.945 --> 00:05:25.965
by fixing the box dimensions to a small set of standard modules that still fit most kitchens

00:05:25.965 --> 00:05:30.885
when you combine them in different ways. That single change would let factories cut panels

00:05:30.885 --> 00:05:36.725
in volume and keep finished units in inventory instead of building to order. The next move would

00:05:36.725 --> 00:05:41.905
treat the cabinet as a structural assembly. Joints would be optimized for automated fastening,

00:05:42.345 --> 00:05:47.205
not for the appearance standards of traditional casework. The architectural woodwork standards

00:05:47.205 --> 00:05:53.265
already lists dowel, dado, and staple joints. Choosing one family and engineering it for speed

00:05:53.265 --> 00:05:58.925
would cut assembly time. Hardware could be standardized across the line, so the same hinge or

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:04.285
slide works in every module. That cuts the stock keeping units that must be stocked and ordered.

00:06:04.905 --> 00:06:10.105
Those steps would require manufacturers to accept a narrower range of finishes and to publish

00:06:10.105 --> 00:06:15.905
exact module dimensions so designers and builders can plan around them. Builders would have to accept

00:06:15.905 --> 00:06:21.585
that some filler panels or scribe pieces will still be needed on irregular walls. The hardest part

00:06:21.585 --> 00:06:27.305
is coordination. Someone has to own the entire chain from panel purchase through final installation

00:06:27.305 --> 00:06:32.845
and warranty, or the savings leak out at each handoff. Where that kind of coordination has

00:06:32.845 --> 00:06:37.925
occurred in other industries, costs have moved closer to the material floor without sacrificing

00:06:37.925 --> 00:06:43.865
basic durability. A further objection is that kitchens contain unique corners, appliance openings,

00:06:44.145 --> 00:06:49.685
and window clearances that resist full standardization. Treat the exceptions as the minority case.

00:06:49.685 --> 00:06:54.445
A core set of repeatable modules could cover the straight runs that make up most of any

00:06:54.445 --> 00:06:54.765
kitchen.

00:06:55.345 --> 00:06:59.285
A smaller set of adjustable fillers and scribe strips would handle the remainder.

00:06:59.985 --> 00:07:01.605
The arithmetic favors that split.

00:07:01.905 --> 00:07:06.585
The majority of cabinet volumes sits in the repeatable sections, so inventory and cutting

00:07:06.585 --> 00:07:09.245
economies would still apply to the bulk of the material.

00:07:09.865 --> 00:07:14.845
The same logic that once collapsed the price of steel, and the cost of moving freight, can

00:07:14.845 --> 00:07:16.825
be applied to the boxes that line a kitchen.

00:07:16.825 --> 00:07:25.845
Watch for two signals. One is a kitchen specified entirely in a handful of repeatable module sizes rather than a custom cut list for every wall.

00:07:26.465 --> 00:07:33.965
The other is a price quoted as a multiple of the delivered panel and hardware cost instead of a lump sum that hides the intermediate markups.

00:07:34.505 --> 00:07:41.725
Who will publish the first set of module dimensions and the corresponding material only cost so the rest of the industry can measure the gap?

00:07:42.345 --> 00:07:44.925
That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown.

00:07:45.445 --> 00:07:47.965
The ideas travel further when you pass them on.

00:07:48.405 --> 00:07:49.725
Send this to a fellow builder.

